Now I see the bug! In `calculate_subimages`:

```c
scanline_width = channels * ihdr->bit_depth;

if(scanline_width > SIZE_MAX / ihdr->width) return SPNG_EOVERFLOW;
scanline_width = scanline_width * sub[i].width;
```

The overflow check uses `ihdr->width` but the multiplication uses `sub[i].width`. For interlaced images, `sub[i].width` can be different from `ihdr->width`. This means the overflow check is incorrect.

But wait, the fuzzer limits width to 200000. Let me think about what values could cause an overflow.

Actually, let me think about this differently. The bug might be simpler. Let me look at the `read_scanline_bytes` function again:

```c
stream->next_in = ctx->data;
```

After `read_idat_bytes` is called, `ctx->data` points to the start of the newly read IDAT data. But `buffer_read_fn` advances `ctx->data` by `ctx->last_read_size`. So after reading the IDAT chunk data, `ctx->data` points to the start of the IDAT data.

But wait, there's a subtle issue. After `inflate` consumes some data, `stream->next_in` and `stream->avail_in` are updated. Then when more data is needed:

```c
ret = read_idat_bytes(ctx, &bytes_read);
if(ret) return ret;

stream->avail_in = bytes_read;
stream->next_in = ctx->data;
```

This sets `stream->next_in` to `ctx->data` which is the start of the newly read data. But `ctx->data` was advanced by `buffer_read_fn` to point to the new data. This seems correct.

Let me think about what happens when the IDAT chunk has a very large length. The `read_idat_bytes` function reads `ctx->current_chunk.length` bytes. But `read_chunk_bytes` checks `if(bytes > ctx->cur_chunk_bytes_left) return 1;`. And `ctx->cur_chunk_bytes_left` is set to `chunk.length` in `read_header`. So this should be fine.

Wait, let me look at the `read_header` function again:

```c
chunk.length = read_u32(ctx->data);

memcpy(&chunk.type, ctx->data + 4, 4);

if(chunk.length > png_u32max) return SPNG_ECHUNK_SIZE;

ctx->cur_chunk_bytes_left = chunk.length;
```

So `chunk.length` is checked against `png_u32max`. But `png_u32max` is probably `UINT32_MAX`. Let me check:

